WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There
are lots of technologies

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:04.590
and communication that go
into building a smart city.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.080
Join us in historic
downtown Austin, Texas,

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.570
as we talk to SoftServe
about using the cloud to make

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:10.980
smarter and safer cities.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.250
So what is a smart city?

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.990
RONALD ESPINOSA: That's the
question of the year, I think.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:23.170
So for years, we had
the notion of "this

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.385
is the year of mobile."

00:00:24.385 --> 00:00:27.010
And it feels like now it's "this
is the year of the smart city"

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.393
for the last couple of years.

00:00:28.393 --> 00:00:30.310
And it means something
different to everybody.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:32.290
I like to think of
them more in terms

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.800
of intelligent environments,
as opposed to smart cities,

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.750
because for one
city, becoming smart

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:42.160
might mean adding lights that
can get brighter or dimmer

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.110
as lighting outside changes.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.290
It could be having
autonomous vehicles.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.620
It could be having
technologies and sensors

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.932
put in place to help
people with disabilities.

00:00:53.932 --> 00:00:55.390
Really, what I
think it is, is it's

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:58.817
an environment that interacts
with the people that are inside

00:00:58.817 --> 00:00:59.650
of that environment.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.240
And that's why I
tend to drift more

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.150
toward an intelligent
environment, where

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.150
that could be a vehicle.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:05.950
It could be a campus.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:07.105
It could be a building.

00:01:07.105 --> 00:01:09.490
It could be the space
that we have right now.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:12.790
If we can be in two
places at the same time,

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.770
physically and
virtually, then I think

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.060
that's a smart environment.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.260
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Have
there been any examples

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.343
that you can talk about
of cities kind of creating

00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:20.385
intelligent environments?

00:01:20.385 --> 00:01:21.301
RONALD ESPINOSA: Sure.

00:01:21.301 --> 00:01:24.050
So in the Carolinas Alliance for
Innovation out of Greenville,

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:28.210
South Carolina, we've got more
into the connected citizen

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.160
in the smart city
environment and

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.010
first-mile last-mile
transportation solves.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.920
And what that is, is what
happens when you have five

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.590
or six different connected
and/or autonomous vehicles--

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:39.850
all different systems--

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.640
needing to speak to each
other, to the environment,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.590
and traffic control
needs to be built?

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.780
And what we were able to
do was create a multi-modal

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.010
transportation, let's
call it, viewpoint--

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.840
single pane of glass,
control tower type of thing--

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.980
where we're able to see not
only where are the vehicles,

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:59.230
but what's the most efficient
mode of transportation,

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.272
from a cost standpoint,
for the connected citizen

00:02:01.272 --> 00:02:03.190
to get from point A to point B?

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.230
And also, what's the
most efficient routing

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:07.443
for emergency services
vehicles and what-have-you?

00:02:07.443 --> 00:02:09.610
Essentially, we're starting
to solve the big problem

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.100
that the public sector has
with digital technologies,

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.870
which is interoperability.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.782
So how does one system
talk to another?

00:02:15.782 --> 00:02:17.740
How do they bi-directionally
inform each other?

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.930
And we've been able to
do all of that in GCP.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:20.740
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
And a lot of that

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.580
is based on, like
you just mentioned,

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.820
getting a lot of data,
being able to collect it.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.340
What does all that technology
look like, especially when

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:28.540
it comes to Google Cloud?

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.998
RONALD ESPINOSA: When you have
just massive amounts of data

00:02:30.998 --> 00:02:34.090
coming in and, really,
new sources of data

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.490
that maybe you hadn't
contemplated before,

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:40.540
it creates piles and piles and
piles of code to go through

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.460
and data files to go through.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.640
And the simple fact is, is that
what used to take years to do,

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.680
can now be done in
minutes with BigQuery.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.180
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So a great example

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.100
of what this might
look like is collecting

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.370
from a whole bunch of
sensors, which is largely IoT.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.080
But like you said,
that could be a tire.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:56.872
That could be cars.

00:02:56.872 --> 00:02:58.080
That could be traffic lights.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.110
That could be anything.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.213
Collecting all that
data, transforming it,

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:03.880
and making those data decisions,
especially the legal ones about

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.860
who owns which data, who
gets visibility into it--

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.190
making sure it all kind of
can play nicely together

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.250
by aggregating it, and then
ultimately doing analysis

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:13.360
on it.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:14.777
RONALD ESPINOSA:
Yeah, I think so.

00:03:14.777 --> 00:03:18.700
And really, I think the key term
here is "enablement" or even

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:19.820
"empowerment."

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:24.050
It's the idea that technology
took off at some point.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.620
And the government was
focused on other things.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.930
And now technology-- that
whole internet thing-- yeah,

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:29.660
it's not a thing.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:30.160
It's here.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:31.670
It's not going away.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.750
And so now the government's in
a position where they say, well,

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:35.720
how do we manage it?

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:36.640
How do we tap into it?

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.400
And I think what's happening is,
Pandora's box has been opened

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.412
and saying, OK, well,
now it's opened.

00:03:41.412 --> 00:03:43.120
Wow, we didn't know
all of this was here.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.370
And as they implement
solutions, like smart lighting,

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:47.470
like smart cars, and
the connected citizen

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:50.353
becomes more and more
pervasive, well, now

00:03:50.353 --> 00:03:51.520
what do we do with all this?

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:52.353
How do we manage it?

00:03:52.353 --> 00:03:54.010
And then as they
drill into it, they

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.260
find out that this
is almost bottomless.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.812
And we have to have something
that scales long-term.

00:03:58.812 --> 00:04:00.520
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Now,
there's a platform

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:02.440
that SoftServe offers
that kind of looks

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:03.520
at all of these aspects.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.030
Can you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.488
RONALD ESPINOSA: So we call it
the intelligent municipality

00:04:07.488 --> 00:04:08.087
platform.

00:04:08.087 --> 00:04:09.670
And essentially,
what that is, is it's

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:14.680
a data store built on GCP
that allows for collection

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.029
from myriad points of data,
export and connections

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:21.910
to any destination
so that we can enable

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:25.180
the public-private partnership
to work together and create

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.380
a digital ecosystem that
sits on top of this.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:30.070
So we start out
with the basics of,

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.900
where is the data coming from?

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.520
How many different
systems do we have?

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.830
How do we put them in play
to work with each other?

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:38.340
And then from there, we
now start to say, OK,

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.680
what are the benefits?

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.630
Well, what if we can distill
things like amber and silver

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:46.450
alerts and get them
to the city corner,

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:49.392
as opposed to the entire zip
code when a message goes out?

00:04:49.392 --> 00:04:50.850
So that's a public
service benefit.

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.180
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah, it
sounds like a lot of this

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.847
wouldn't have been
possible without some

00:04:53.847 --> 00:04:56.320
of the advancements in the
cloud, especially recently.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.210
RONALD ESPINOSA: I
think 100% impossible.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.880
Where would you ever put
enough computing power

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:01.840
to make it happen?

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So both
because of scale and because

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:05.507
of the technologies
being offered,

00:05:05.507 --> 00:05:07.122
but also some of the new--

00:05:07.122 --> 00:05:08.830
like you mentioned
earlier about BigQuery

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:12.670
and being able to quickly
analyze huge amounts of data

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:13.622
very rapidly--

00:05:13.622 --> 00:05:15.580
all of these things have
kind of enabled people

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.557
to ask, how can I improve?

00:05:17.557 --> 00:05:18.640
Where can I use this data?

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:20.033
And how can I analyze it?

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:21.200
RONALD ESPINOSA: I think so.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.310
I also think that the ease
of use, ease of access,

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:26.320
and, in a way--

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.480
and I'll use this
term abstractly--

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:29.770
a bit of standards.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:33.760
There is a certain understanding
of what the Google Cloud

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:35.290
Platform is.

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:38.120
Use these tools to
do these things.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.870
And you can have people
coming out of school,

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.547
ready to jump in and adopt this.

00:05:42.547 --> 00:05:44.380
Well, that takes a lot
of the fear out of it

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:46.730
for a public sector entity,
and really for anyone.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:48.190
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well, thanks
so much for coming in, Ron,

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:50.680
and telling us about how
SoftServe helps enable

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.690
companies, including
the public sector,

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.732
to really understand and
do more with their data.

00:05:54.732 --> 00:05:56.898
RONALD ESPINOSA: Sure, I
appreciate the opportunity.

00:05:56.898 --> 00:05:57.706
Thank you.

00:05:57.706 --> 00:05:58.733
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:58.733 --> 00:06:00.400
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Thanks for joining us,

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.290
and stay tuned for more customer
interviews on Stack Chat.

